1
00:00:01,660 --> 00:00:04,360
弗里曼:每个人都有一个起点.
Freeman: Everyone has a beginning.

2
00:00:04,360 --> 00:00:07,560
[ 婴儿哭声 ]
[ Baby crying ]

3
00:00:07,570 --> 00:00:10,000
什么时候才算这个起点的开始呢？
But when does that beginning begin？

4
00:00:11,800 --> 00:00:15,610
是两个细胞融合的瞬间？
Is it the instant two cells fuse together？

5
00:00:17,170 --> 00:00:20,340
还是我们出现在这个世界上的时候？
Or the moment we enter the world？

6
00:00:20,350 --> 00:00:23,610
科学家和宗教领袖对于
Scientists and religious leaders don't agree

7
00:00:23,620 --> 00:00:27,480
何时首次出现生命的火花仍有分歧.
on when the first spark of life occurs.

8
00:00:27,490 --> 00:00:29,550
生命是仅仅的生物学过程,
Is life just biology

9
00:00:29,550 --> 00:00:32,160
还是我们的意识感
or does our sense of consciousness

10
00:00:32,160 --> 00:00:35,260
决定了我们是否实在的活着？
dictate whether we are truly alive？

11
00:00:36,790 --> 00:00:39,430
如果我们能够创造意识,
If we can create consciousness,

12
00:00:39,430 --> 00:00:46,200
我们能否在非生命躯体内创造生命,来了解何时生命才算诞生？
can we build life out of nonlife and understand when life begins？

13
00:00:52,210 --> 00:00:57,210
空间、时间、生命本身
Space,time,life itself.

14
00:00:59,350 --> 00:01:03,590
宇宙的奥秘，尽在穿越虫洞
The secrets of the cosmos lie through the wormhole.

15
00:01:07,610 --> 00:01:13,810
♪ 生命何时才算诞生？ ♪ 
♪  When Does Life Begin？  ♪  首播于 2013.6.5  ♪ 

16
00:01:16,470 --> 00:01:19,470
生命是一个奇迹.
Life is a miracle.

17
00:01:19,470 --> 00:01:23,070
这个词经常被用于让我们感动的时候,
That's a word we use when we are moved by something

18
00:01:23,070 --> 00:01:26,340
或是在我们不知道发生了什么的时候.
and when we don't understand how it happens.

19
00:01:26,340 --> 00:01:29,210
一个孩子出生前的九个月,
Nine months before a child is born,

20
00:01:29,210 --> 00:01:31,250
仅仅是少量的细胞,
it's just a handful of cells,

21
00:01:31,250 --> 00:01:33,220
很明显,并不比生活在
no more complex,apparently,

22
00:01:33,220 --> 00:01:36,190
我们皮肤上的细菌复杂多少.
than the bacteria that live on our skin.

23
00:01:36,190 --> 00:01:40,060
这些细胞团都拥有基因,
Both these clumps of cells have genes.

24
00:01:40,060 --> 00:01:42,060
都可以增殖,
They both reproduce.

25
00:01:42,060 --> 00:01:45,760
只是一个变成了我们认为非常有价值的东西,
One turns into something we value greatly.

26
00:01:45,760 --> 00:01:48,560
另一个会让我们得病.
The other just makes us sick.

27
00:01:48,570 --> 00:01:51,300
但它们都是奇迹--
But they are both miracles --

28
00:01:51,300 --> 00:01:56,340
擦出生命神秘火花的化学反应的集合.
a collection of chemicals with a mysterious spark of life.

29
00:01:58,180 --> 00:02:01,580
小时候你是否为自己做过午餐？
Did you ever make your own lunches growing up？

30
00:02:01,580 --> 00:02:04,180
我一般都会做花生黄油三明治.
I used to make peanut butter sandwiches.

31
00:02:04,180 --> 00:02:08,720
有时候,我发现面包上有发霉的部分,
Every once in a while,I'd find a moldy slice of bread.

32
00:02:08,720 --> 00:02:12,260
我想,“这些绿色绒毛来自哪里呢？”,
"Where did this green fuzz come from？" I wondered.

33
00:02:12,260 --> 00:02:16,160
看起来似乎是从空气中来,
Just seemed to have appeared from thin air.

34
00:02:16,160 --> 00:02:19,430
我好奇如果我不管它的话会发生什么.
I wondered what would happen if I left it alone.

35
00:02:19,430 --> 00:02:23,830
这些霉会继续生长成为一个毛绒的霉菌怪兽吗？
Would the mold keep growing into a fuzzy mold monster？

36
00:02:29,740 --> 00:02:31,370
但它不会.
It didn't.

37
00:02:31,380 --> 00:02:36,210
它是活的,但不是我幻想的那样.
It was alive,but not in the way I was.

38
00:02:36,210 --> 00:02:41,550
那我身体内有什么能让我成长为一个男孩？
What was it inside me that made me grow into a boy？

39
00:02:48,730 --> 00:02:51,190
Maureen Condic是一名生物学家,
Maureen Condic is a biologist

40
00:02:51,200 --> 00:02:54,130
就职于犹他大学医学院.
at the University of Utah School of Medicine.

41
00:02:54,130 --> 00:02:57,230
她花了整个周末
She spends her weekends traversing

42
00:02:57,240 --> 00:02:59,500
横跨犹他州瓦萨奇山脉,
the Wasatch Mountains of Utah,

43
00:02:59,500 --> 00:03:03,310
这里生命繁茂.
where life springs up all around her.

44
00:03:03,310 --> 00:03:04,540
Condic: 作为一名生物学家,
Condic: As a biologist,

45
00:03:04,540 --> 00:03:06,040
来到这些山脉中总是很吸引人,
it's always fascinating to come to the mountains,

46
00:03:06,040 --> 00:03:09,110
因为在你身边会有如此多样的生命,
because there's such a diversity of life around you,

47
00:03:09,110 --> 00:03:14,150
而它们又以完全不同的方式成长为现在这样.
and it all comes into being in radically different ways.

48
00:03:14,150 --> 00:03:16,790
弗里曼：所有的生物体都会繁殖,
Freeman: All organisms reproduce,

49
00:03:16,790 --> 00:03:19,690
但它们都以不同的方式繁殖.
but they have different ways of doing it.

50
00:03:19,690 --> 00:03:22,890
白杨树通过复制自身
The Aspen trees that pervade the mountains of Utah

51
00:03:22,890 --> 00:03:24,660
遍布犹他山脉.
clone themselves.

52
00:03:24,660 --> 00:03:30,170
它们的后代从成熟树木的根部发出芽,
Offspring grow as shoots from the roots of more mature trees,

53
00:03:30,170 --> 00:03:33,200
接着长成同样的树木,
creating growths that are really the same tree

54
00:03:33,200 --> 00:03:36,040
这样不停的重复.
grown over and over again.

55
00:03:36,040 --> 00:03:38,410
另一方面,一些虫子,
Some worms,on the other hand,

56
00:03:38,410 --> 00:03:41,640
则以不同的方式创造它们的下一代.
create their next generations in a different way.

57
00:03:43,550 --> 00:03:46,350
Condic: 有一个完整的纲概括了所谓的涡虫【涡虫纲】,
Condic: There's a whole class of worms known as planaria.

58
00:03:46,350 --> 00:03:49,120
它们的繁殖是通过将身体背面贴在石头上,
They reproduce by attaching the back of their body to a rock

59
00:03:49,120 --> 00:03:51,190
把自己的身体伸长,
and stretching themselves out

60
00:03:51,190 --> 00:03:53,390
直到把自己撕裂成两个,
until they literally tear themselves in two.

61
00:03:53,390 --> 00:03:57,430
接着这两个一半就会发育成一个完整的虫子.
And then each of the two halves produce a full worm.

62
00:03:57,430 --> 00:04:00,800
弗里曼：大自然为生命的开始创造了无数种方式,
Freeman: Nature offers myriad ways for life to begin.

63
00:04:01,730 --> 00:04:05,130
但使Maureen着迷的是一种
But the one that fascinates Maureen

64
00:04:05,140 --> 00:04:07,840
发生在你和我身上的繁殖方式.
is the way it happens for you and me.

65
00:04:07,840 --> 00:04:12,110
这当然不会涉及到鹳.
And,no,it doesn't involve a stork.

66
00:04:12,110 --> 00:04:14,110
Condic: 人类生命的存在
Condic: So,human life comes into existence

67
00:04:14,110 --> 00:04:16,580
发生在短短的一瞬.
in just a fraction of a second.

68
00:04:16,580 --> 00:04:18,580
一个人类卵细胞和一个人类精子,
You have a human egg and a human sperm,

69
00:04:18,580 --> 00:04:21,520
它们存活的唯一目的就是找到对方并融合,
and their sole purpose in life is to find each other and fuse.

70
00:04:21,520 --> 00:04:24,150
它们结合到一起,就在这一瞬间,
So,they come together,and in that one instant,

71
00:04:24,160 --> 00:04:29,190
就诞生了一个新的细胞--一个单细胞的人类胚胎.
you create a new kind of cell -- a one-celled human embryo.

72
00:04:29,190 --> 00:04:33,700
弗里曼：这个新细胞拥有它自己的遗传信息,DNA,
Freeman: This new cell has its own genetic code,its own DNA,

73
00:04:33,700 --> 00:04:38,230
是卵细胞和精子组成的唯一的一个复合体.
that is a unique mixture of the egg and the sperm.

74
00:04:38,240 --> 00:04:39,940
Condic: 这个单个细胞,
Condic: And within this single cell,

75
00:04:39,940 --> 00:04:41,840
就有成长发育的完整计划.
there's a complete plan for development.

76
00:04:41,840 --> 00:04:43,970
成长过程并不创造新的遗传信息,
Development doesn't create that information.

77
00:04:43,980 --> 00:04:45,610
遗传信息的创造就在精卵结合的开始.
It's there from the very beginning.

78
00:04:45,610 --> 00:04:49,650
弗里曼: 从她对胚胎学的了解出发,
Freeman: From her understanding of embryology,

79
00:04:49,650 --> 00:04:53,780
Maureen认为一个人类个体的生命
Maureen concludes that the life of a unique human individual

80
00:04:53,790 --> 00:04:56,190
出现在1/4秒内,
begins within the 1/4 of a second

81
00:04:56,190 --> 00:05:00,560
就是精子和卵细胞结合的时间.
it takes the sperm and the egg to unite.

82
00:05:00,560 --> 00:05:02,530
这个单细胞胚胎
That single-celled embryo

83
00:05:02,530 --> 00:05:05,430
包含了一套复杂的发育指南,
contains an elaborate instruction manual

84
00:05:05,430 --> 00:05:11,170
即成长为一个人类所需的所有信息.
with all of the information needed to create a human being.

85
00:05:11,170 --> 00:05:12,640
你可以想象成
You can think of it like

86
00:05:12,640 --> 00:05:18,210
一个可以自由组装的帐篷.
a camping tent that builds itself.

87
00:05:18,210 --> 00:05:20,240
那么我们的帐篷可以自我组装,
So,our tent is self-assembling because it has

88
00:05:20,250 --> 00:05:23,050
因为它的组成部分全放在这里了,
all of the parts it needs to put itself together

89
00:05:23,050 --> 00:05:26,650
而且还有一套说明书,
and because it has a set of instructions built into it

90
00:05:26,650 --> 00:05:30,620
能够将其组装成最终状态.
that allow it to assemble into its final state.

91
00:05:30,620 --> 00:05:32,560
弗里曼：生命就像这个帐篷,
Freeman: Life is like this tent,

92
00:05:32,560 --> 00:05:37,690
只是比它复杂十亿倍.
only a billion times more complex.

93
00:05:37,700 --> 00:05:41,600
在单细胞胚胎形成后,
After the single-celled embryo is formed,

94
00:05:41,600 --> 00:05:43,930
它就随着输卵管向下移动,
it takes a journey down the fallopian tubes

95
00:05:43,940 --> 00:05:45,130
进入子宫,
to the uterus,

96
00:05:45,140 --> 00:05:48,670
在这里它开始了细胞分裂,
where it begins the process of cell division,

97
00:05:48,670 --> 00:05:53,140
从2个细胞到4个,再到8个,到16个这样...
from 2 cells to 4 to 8 to 16 and so on.

98
00:05:53,140 --> 00:05:54,610
大约一周之后,
After about one week,

99
00:05:54,610 --> 00:05:57,710
胚胎到达了子宫壁,
the embryo arrives at the uterine wall

100
00:05:57,720 --> 00:05:59,880
并将自己埋藏于其中.
and implants itself.

101
00:05:59,880 --> 00:06:02,820
一周后,胚胎中的细胞
A week later,the cells of the embryo

102
00:06:02,820 --> 00:06:07,620
开始重排成为原始的身体.
begin to reorganize themselves into a primitive body.

103
00:06:07,630 --> 00:06:09,230
三周后,
After three weeks,

104
00:06:09,230 --> 00:06:12,560
神经系统开始出现,
the beginning of the nervous system is in place.

105
00:06:12,560 --> 00:06:16,400
随着时间的推移,细胞持续复制,
As days go on,cells continue to multiply,

106
00:06:16,400 --> 00:06:20,570
形成血管,心脏开始跳动,
blood vessels form,the heart starts to beat,

107
00:06:20,570 --> 00:06:22,740
脊椎骨开始成形,
the backbone takes shape,

108
00:06:22,740 --> 00:06:25,910
手臂和腿开始膨出.
arms and legs begin to bulge out.

109
00:06:25,910 --> 00:06:27,540
这个时候,
During that time,

110
00:06:27,550 --> 00:06:31,480
脑开始形成.
the formation of the brain begins.

111
00:06:31,480 --> 00:06:33,520
Condic: 胚胎的细胞和组织
Condic: The cells and tissues of the embryo

112
00:06:33,520 --> 00:06:36,820
通过这些神奇的复杂细胞行为,
undergo these amazingly complex cellular gymnastics

113
00:06:36,820 --> 00:06:39,660
形成了不同结构和器官,
to give rise to structures,organs,

114
00:06:39,660 --> 00:06:42,960
它们之间的相互关系使得简单的胚胎
complex relationships that turn that flat embryo

115
00:06:42,960 --> 00:06:46,430
变成了它形成的复杂的东西.
into something that has form.

116
00:06:52,270 --> 00:06:54,700
弗里曼：Maureen对人类胚胎的
Freeman: Maureen's biological understanding

117
00:06:54,710 --> 00:06:56,640
生长发育的生物学见解,
of the development of a human embryo

118
00:06:56,640 --> 00:06:58,510
使得她确定什么时候才是
leaves her with little doubt

119
00:06:58,510 --> 00:07:02,810
一个新的人类生命的产生.
about when a new human life begins.

120
00:07:02,810 --> 00:07:07,220
我们全都可以将自身的起源追溯到这个
We all trace our own origins back to this single cell

121
00:07:07,220 --> 00:07:09,820
通过精卵融合产生的单细胞.
that came into existence at sperm-egg fusion.

122
00:07:09,820 --> 00:07:12,760
这正是我们应该尝试理解的
It's something we should try to understand

123
00:07:12,760 --> 00:07:14,460
作为自我发现的过程,
as a process of self-discovery,

124
00:07:14,460 --> 00:07:17,130
认识我们本质的过程.
as a process of understanding our natures.

125
00:07:17,130 --> 00:07:18,830
这就是我们的开始.
This is where we began.

126
00:07:18,830 --> 00:07:22,730
弗里曼：但并不是所有的科学家都将个体起源
Freeman: But not all scientists trace an individual's origins

127
00:07:22,730 --> 00:07:25,600
归于两个细胞的融合.
back to just two cells.

128
00:07:27,600 --> 00:07:32,310
事实上,一些科学家认为一个个体中,
In fact,some scientists believe within one individual

129
00:07:32,310 --> 00:07:35,980
可以存在多个生命的痕迹,
there could be the ghosts of multiple lives,

130
00:07:35,980 --> 00:07:38,210
这也是地球上生命一代到另一代的
that life on earth is an unbreakable chain of events

131
00:07:38,220 --> 00:07:41,650
牢不可破的混合在一起的
where one generation and the next

132
00:07:41,650 --> 00:07:44,050
一串事件.
are blurred together.

133
00:07:44,060 --> 00:07:47,160
Gammill: 还是孩子的时候,我会去看我的爷爷,
Gammill: When I was a kid,I would go and visit my grandfather,

134
00:07:47,160 --> 00:07:49,460
他经常下象棋,我就会过去看他下棋,
and he used to play chess,and I would go and watch him,

135
00:07:49,460 --> 00:07:51,860
我长大后就跟着他学习下棋...
and I grew up learning to play with him...

136
00:07:51,860 --> 00:07:52,860
走“卒”到G-4.
Pawn to G-4.

137
00:07:56,200 --> 00:07:58,030
...然后我父亲也教我儿子下棋,
...and then my father also taught my son,

138
00:07:58,040 --> 00:07:59,670
现在我儿子和我在下棋.
and now my son and I play.

139
00:08:03,070 --> 00:08:04,410
[失真的声音] 将军.
[ Distorted voice ] Checkmate.

140
00:08:04,410 --> 00:08:07,640
我认为类似于象棋从一代传向另一代,
I think there's an interesting parallel between chess

141
00:08:07,650 --> 00:08:09,750
还有一些东西也可以传向下一代,
that was passed from one generation to another,

142
00:08:09,750 --> 00:08:11,850
我们现在知道是生物学上的
and what we know biologically now

143
00:08:11,850 --> 00:08:14,580
两代人之间的细胞传递.
is the passage of cells between generations.

144
00:08:17,120 --> 00:08:19,120
弗里曼：Hilary Gammill医生
Freeman: Dr. Hilary Gammill

145
00:08:19,120 --> 00:08:21,890
来自弗雷德·哈钦森癌症研究中心,
from the Fred Hutchinson Cancer Research Center

146
00:08:21,890 --> 00:08:23,630
她认为个体生命
believes individual lives

147
00:08:23,630 --> 00:08:26,600
交织的东西比我们想象的还要多.
are more interwoven than we ever imagined.

148
00:08:27,830 --> 00:08:30,500
她在思考我们所有人体内
She wonders if inside all of us

149
00:08:30,500 --> 00:08:32,840
是否会有我们亲人的痕迹,
there are traces of our relatives,

150
00:08:32,840 --> 00:08:35,940
甚至可以追溯到几代人之前.
dating back generations.

151
00:08:35,940 --> 00:08:38,310
Gammill: 我研究的领域集中在母亲在怀孕期
Gammill: My area of focus is on the exchange of cells

152
00:08:38,310 --> 00:08:40,740
是否会和胎儿存在细胞交换.
between a mother and fetus during pregnancy.

153
00:08:40,750 --> 00:08:42,480
经典教科书中说
The classic textbooks used to state

154
00:08:42,480 --> 00:08:44,280
胎儿血液和母体血液
that there was absolutely no contact

155
00:08:44,280 --> 00:08:46,250
绝对不会存在相互接触--
between the fetal blood and the maternal blood --

156
00:08:46,250 --> 00:08:48,580
两人的血液都只会分隔地保存在自己体内.
they were just protected in separate compartments.

157
00:08:48,590 --> 00:08:52,890
弗里曼：许多年来,科学家认为胎盘形成后,
Freeman: For years,scientists thought when the placenta forms,

158
00:08:52,890 --> 00:08:55,560
就形成了母亲和胎儿之间
it acts as an impenetrable barrier

159
00:08:55,560 --> 00:08:57,830
不可逾越的屏障,
between the mother and the fetus,

160
00:08:57,830 --> 00:08:59,360
像携带有外来基因的
so nothing as large

161
00:08:59,360 --> 00:09:01,330
外来细胞这么大的东西
as a foreign cell with foreign genes

162
00:09:01,330 --> 00:09:03,200
是不可能穿过胎盘的--
could pass through --

163
00:09:03,200 --> 00:09:06,140
婴儿的DNA就是他/她自己的DNA,
baby's DNA stays within the baby,

164
00:09:06,140 --> 00:09:09,240
母亲的DNA就是母亲自己的.
and mom's DNA stays with the mom.

165
00:09:13,740 --> 00:09:16,780
通过对母亲血流的观察,
By peering into the bloodstreams of mothers,

166
00:09:16,780 --> 00:09:20,650
Hilary和她的团队发现一些不可思议的东西.
Hilary and her team have discovered something remarkable.

167
00:09:20,650 --> 00:09:23,350
当他们从母亲的血液中
When they sampled just a teaspoon

168
00:09:23,350 --> 00:09:25,250
提取一茶匙量的样品,
of blood from the mothers,

169
00:09:25,260 --> 00:09:29,160
他们发现了非常多的流动的外来细胞,
they found dozens and dozens of foreign cells floating around,

170
00:09:29,160 --> 00:09:32,290
这些细胞来自于她们体内的孩子.
cells from the mothers' babies.

171
00:09:33,630 --> 00:09:37,030
我们现在了解的是通过怀孕,
So,we actually understand now that throughout pregnancy,

172
00:09:37,030 --> 00:09:40,570
会在母体和胎儿之间存在
there is bidirectional exchange of information cells and DNA

173
00:09:40,570 --> 00:09:42,170
细胞和DNA的双向信息交换.
between the mother and the fetus.

174
00:09:42,170 --> 00:09:48,410
弗里曼：Hilary的研究表明了胎盘并不是不可穿透的,
Freeman: Hilary's work shows the placenta is not at all impenetrable.

175
00:09:48,410 --> 00:09:50,610
它更像是个筛子,
It's more like a sieve.

176
00:09:50,620 --> 00:09:52,050
上面有很多微孔,
There are miniscule holes

177
00:09:52,050 --> 00:09:54,980
可以使细胞进出.
that let cells out and let cells in.

178
00:09:54,990 --> 00:09:59,390
这些外来细胞可以在我们体内存活数十年.
These foreign cells can survive in our bodies for decades.

179
00:09:59,390 --> 00:10:00,920
这种交换不仅仅
It's an exchange that happens

180
00:10:00,930 --> 00:10:03,030
发生在母亲和胎儿之间,
not only between mother and fetus.

181
00:10:03,030 --> 00:10:06,700
来自其他亲人的细胞可能也会溜进去.
Cells from other relatives may sneak in,too.

182
00:10:06,700 --> 00:10:10,370
理论上我们可能拥有
It is theoretically possible that we could have cells

183
00:10:10,370 --> 00:10:12,500
通过许多不同来源交换的细胞--
that are exchanged from multiple different sources --

184
00:10:12,500 --> 00:10:14,640
比如哥哥或姐姐,
you know,older siblings

185
00:10:14,640 --> 00:10:16,770
当然还有母亲和胎儿的,
and certainly mothers and fetuses,

186
00:10:16,770 --> 00:10:19,440
甚至过去几代人的.
but past generations,as well.

187
00:10:19,440 --> 00:10:23,310
弗里曼：当一位母亲获得来自胎儿的细胞时,
Freeman: When a mother acquires cells from a fetus,

188
00:10:23,310 --> 00:10:25,410
她可以将这些细胞传递到她
she could pass on these baby cells

189
00:10:25,420 --> 00:10:26,720
下一个孩子.
to her next child.

190
00:10:26,720 --> 00:10:28,820
弟弟或者妹妹就可能拥有
A younger sibling could have cells

191
00:10:28,820 --> 00:10:31,820
来自哥哥或姐姐体内的细胞.
from the body of an older sibling.

192
00:10:31,820 --> 00:10:34,990
这些细胞不仅仅是因为好奇,
These cells are more than just a curiosity.

193
00:10:34,990 --> 00:10:36,990
它们可以成为体内的士兵,
They can act as soldiers in the body

194
00:10:36,990 --> 00:10:39,430
与疾病战斗...
and combat disease...

195
00:10:39,430 --> 00:10:42,060
这些细胞可以在接受者体内
Those cells can be active against cancer cells

196
00:10:42,070 --> 00:10:43,300
激活来抵御癌症细胞.
that develop in the recipient.

197
00:10:43,300 --> 00:10:46,140
弗里曼：...但不是所有交换的细胞
Freeman: ...But not all of these exchanged cells

198
00:10:46,140 --> 00:10:48,000
都可以保护防御机体.
protect and defend.

199
00:10:48,010 --> 00:10:49,410
当我们的免疫系统
When our immune system

200
00:10:49,410 --> 00:10:51,870
检测到血液中这些外来细胞,
detects these foreign cells in the bloodstream,

201
00:10:51,880 --> 00:10:55,480
就可能会攻击它们.
it may decide to attack them.

202
00:10:55,480 --> 00:10:58,150
Gammill: 另外这些少量的在个体内
Gammill: These small numbers of foreign cells

203
00:10:58,150 --> 00:10:59,720
持续存在的外来细胞
that are persistent in an individual

204
00:10:59,720 --> 00:11:02,520
还与疾病相关,
are associated with disease states,

205
00:11:02,520 --> 00:11:05,190
比如自身免疫病、系统性硬化病.
like autoimmune diseases like systemic sclerosis.

206
00:11:06,590 --> 00:11:08,290
细胞交换
The exchange of cells

207
00:11:08,290 --> 00:11:11,260
对于个体的健康而言,
may have both positive and negative consequences

208
00:11:11,260 --> 00:11:13,700
既有好的一面,也会有不好的结果
for the health of the individuals involved.

209
00:11:16,170 --> 00:11:18,330
弗里曼：来自我们亲人的细胞的存在,
Freeman: The presence of cells from our relatives

210
00:11:18,340 --> 00:11:21,000
会改变我们的整个生命,
could change the course of our entire lives,

211
00:11:21,010 --> 00:11:24,270
使我们之间的相互联系
making us all more interconnected

212
00:11:24,280 --> 00:11:26,510
比我们曾经认为的还要密切.
than we ever thought possible.

213
00:11:26,510 --> 00:11:29,310
Gammill: 了解细胞在不同个体间的
Gammill: Understanding that cells are exchanged

214
00:11:29,310 --> 00:11:32,480
一般交换,
commonly between individuals,

215
00:11:32,480 --> 00:11:35,650
我认为,使个体间的界线不再清晰,
I think,blurs the borders between those individuals

216
00:11:35,650 --> 00:11:37,550
这样一个生命的起始,
so that the beginning of one life

217
00:11:37,560 --> 00:11:40,460
与另一个生命的终结就不再那么清楚.
and the end of another life are a little bit less clear.

218
00:11:42,290 --> 00:11:44,960
弗里曼：我们身体的一些部分
Freeman: The life of some parts of our bodies

219
00:11:44,960 --> 00:11:48,900
的确产生于精卵融合之前,
actually begins before our sperm and egg have ever met,

220
00:11:48,900 --> 00:11:51,030
但斯德哥尔摩的一位医生
but one doctor in Stockholm

221
00:11:51,040 --> 00:11:52,900
想要将生命的起始
wants to push the beginning of life

222
00:11:52,900 --> 00:11:54,340
推向另一个方向.
in the other direction.

223
00:11:54,340 --> 00:12:00,980
他认为在我们知道自己活着之前都不算活着.
He believes we cannot be alive until we know we are alive.

224
00:12:03,460 --> 00:12:05,500
那到底什么才算人类生命的开端？
When does human life begin？

225
00:12:06,730 --> 00:12:09,300
在这个可以进行体外受精
In this age of in vitro fertilization

226
00:12:09,300 --> 00:12:10,840
和围产医学的时代,
【围产医学：在产前对孕妇和胎儿进行监护.】
and prenatal medicine,

227
00:12:10,840 --> 00:12:14,010
这的确是科学家和医生
it's a question that scientists and doctors

228
00:12:14,010 --> 00:12:15,840
每天都在努力解答的问题.
struggle to answer every day.

229
00:12:17,010 --> 00:12:20,050
也许有另一种方法可以解答这个问题.
Perhaps there's another way to approach the question.

230
00:12:20,050 --> 00:12:23,480
你如何知道是否有人在家呢？
How do you know if someone is home？

231
00:12:23,480 --> 00:12:28,820
你会敲门,看是否会有人应门.
You knock on the door,see if someone answers.

232
00:12:31,660 --> 00:12:34,630
当Hugo Lagercrantz医生还是个年轻人时,
When Dr. Hugo Lagercrantz was a younger man,

233
00:12:34,630 --> 00:12:37,000
他的生活很有压力.
his life was stressful.

234
00:12:38,330 --> 00:12:40,300
[ Baby crying ]
[ Baby crying ]

235
00:12:42,740 --> 00:12:45,470
他是位于斯德哥尔摩的
He was the director of the neonatal intensive care unit

236
00:12:45,470 --> 00:12:47,670
阿斯特丽德·林格伦儿童医院
at the Astrid Lindgren Children's Hospital

237
00:12:47,670 --> 00:12:49,470
新生儿重症监护室的主管,
in Stockholm,

238
00:12:49,480 --> 00:12:53,410
在这里他监护着胎儿和新生儿.
where he monitored fetuses and newborn babies.

239
00:12:53,410 --> 00:12:56,180
当时胎儿监护还是非常新的技术,
Fetal monitoring was quite new at that time.

240
00:12:56,180 --> 00:12:57,380
会有很多的误报,
There were a lot of false alarms,

241
00:12:57,380 --> 00:12:59,680
所以[笑]这很让人头疼.
so [chuckles] it was distressing.

242
00:12:59,690 --> 00:13:03,090
弗里曼：但是Hugo的病人遭受的是
Freeman: But Hugo's patients experienced far more stress

243
00:13:03,090 --> 00:13:05,120
远比他多的压力--
than he ever did --

244
00:13:05,130 --> 00:13:08,190
不是母亲,而是孩子.
not the mothers,the babies.

245
00:13:08,200 --> 00:13:12,530
Lagercrantz: 被生出来是一生中最困难的事件,
Lagercrantz: Being born is the most stressful event in life,

246
00:13:12,530 --> 00:13:15,000
尤其是以自然的方式生产出来.
particularly if you're born in the natural way.

247
00:13:16,400 --> 00:13:18,000
弗里曼：我们出生的时候,
Freeman: When we are born,

248
00:13:18,010 --> 00:13:20,370
会被从温暖安全的子宫中取出,
we're taken from our warm,safe womb

249
00:13:20,370 --> 00:13:22,770
扔进这个世界中.
and thrown into the world.

250
00:13:23,940 --> 00:13:27,750
这也是我们生命中最重要的一天,
It's the most dramatic day of our lives,

251
00:13:27,750 --> 00:13:32,680
但这天我们甚至都记不得.
but it's a day we don't even remember.

252
00:13:35,320 --> 00:13:36,960
这就让Hugo思考,
This made Hugo wonder,

253
00:13:36,960 --> 00:13:40,160
“婴儿什么时候才会意识到发生了什么呢？
"When do babies become aware of what's going on？

254
00:13:40,160 --> 00:13:42,860
意识什么时候才会产生？”
When does consciousness begin？"

255
00:13:42,860 --> 00:13:48,370
他认为我们可以通过鱼类寻找线索.
He thinks we can look for clues by looking at fish.

256
00:13:48,370 --> 00:13:50,740
Lagercrantz: 现在瑞典出台了一项新法律,
Lagercrantz: Now there is a new law in Sweden

257
00:13:50,740 --> 00:13:53,610
规定人们钓鱼的时候不允许使用鱼钩.
that you're not allowed to use hooks when you're fishing.

258
00:13:53,610 --> 00:13:55,310
这可能是因为他们认为
The idea is that they think

259
00:13:55,310 --> 00:13:57,910
鱼类也会有意识并感到痛苦,
that the fish may be conscious and suffer,

260
00:13:57,910 --> 00:14:00,350
当然它们也可以对痛觉产生反应,
and certainly the fish reacts to pain,

261
00:14:00,350 --> 00:14:03,620
但是我不觉得鱼类会有意识的感觉到痛苦,
but I don't think the fish is conscious about pain.

262
00:14:03,620 --> 00:14:07,590
这更像是一种反射.
It's more kind of a reflex.

263
00:14:09,060 --> 00:14:10,620
弗里曼：根据Hugo的说法,
Freeman: According to Hugo,

264
00:14:10,620 --> 00:14:14,130
鱼类无法体验到心理层面的痛苦,
fish cannot experience the psychological aspect of pain

265
00:14:14,130 --> 00:14:16,260
因为它们缺乏相关的脑部回路,
because they lack the brain circuitry

266
00:14:16,260 --> 00:14:18,400
这个回路被称作“丘脑皮层联结”. 【丘脑皮层联结：从皮层下结构
(如丘脑(thalamus))到皮层的投射】
called thalamocortical connections.

267
00:14:18,400 --> 00:14:19,900
谢谢.
Thank you.

268
00:14:22,300 --> 00:14:24,040
丘脑皮层联结的运作方式
Thalamocortical connections

269
00:14:24,040 --> 00:14:26,570
像是人脑内的交换机.
operate like a switchboard in the human brain.

270
00:14:26,570 --> 00:14:32,740
我们看、听、碰、闻或尝到东西,
Whenever we see,hear,touch,smell,or taste something,

271
00:14:32,750 --> 00:14:36,750
电信号就从感觉器官传到大脑皮层,
electrical signals go from our sensory organs to the cortex

272
00:14:36,750 --> 00:14:41,690
这样我们的脑就可以处理我们在这个世界里经历了什么.
so our brains can process what we experience in the world.

273
00:14:41,690 --> 00:14:44,260
这些丘脑皮层联结
These thalamocortical connections

274
00:14:44,260 --> 00:14:46,460
对于意识是非常重要的,
are crucial for consciousness

275
00:14:46,460 --> 00:14:48,560
因为我们认为意识--
because we believe that consciousness --

276
00:14:48,560 --> 00:14:53,470
至少是高等意识--就位于皮层中,
at least high consciousness -- is localized in the cortex,

277
00:14:53,470 --> 00:14:57,470
如果你看到、听到、感觉到的
and if what you see,what you hear,what you sense

278
00:14:57,470 --> 00:15:00,110
无法到达皮层,
doesn't reach the cortex,

279
00:15:00,110 --> 00:15:01,610
那么你就意识不到它.
then you cannot be conscious about it.

280
00:15:01,610 --> 00:15:05,180
弗里曼：Hugo现在是一位新生儿研究员,
Freeman: Hugo is now a neonatal researcher

281
00:15:05,180 --> 00:15:08,280
研究人类的脑何时会发育出这些联结.
and studies when human brains develop these connections.

282
00:15:09,450 --> 00:15:13,790
什么时候产生了意识的第一簇火花？
When does the first spark of consciousness happen？

283
00:15:13,790 --> 00:15:17,960
Donato是一个4天大的足月婴儿,
Donato,a 4-day-old full-term baby,

284
00:15:17,960 --> 00:15:20,890
他可以帮助Hugo发现答案.
is here to help Hugo find out.

285
00:15:20,890 --> 00:15:24,460
关于意识有几个标准,
There are several criteria for consciousness.

286
00:15:24,460 --> 00:15:26,430
一个是处于清醒状态,
One is being awake

287
00:15:26,430 --> 00:15:30,030
然后能够意识到自己的身体,
and then to be aware of your body

288
00:15:30,040 --> 00:15:32,840
再然后就是能够意识到你所看到、
and then,of course,to be aware what you see

289
00:15:32,840 --> 00:15:35,170
听到和闻到的等等.
and hear and smell,et cetera.

290
00:15:35,180 --> 00:15:38,810
弗里曼：Hugo和他的研究小组将Donate与
Freeman: Hugo and his team connect Donato to an instrument

291
00:15:38,810 --> 00:15:40,780
一个可以测量他脑中的血流的仪器相连.
that measures blood flow in his brain.

292
00:15:40,780 --> 00:15:42,210
[监视器哔哔声]
[ Monitor beeping ]

293
00:15:43,950 --> 00:15:46,580
当给予Donato特定的刺激时,
When Donato is exposed to certain stimuli,

294
00:15:46,590 --> 00:15:48,320
Hugo就能知道
Hugo can tell

295
00:15:48,320 --> 00:15:50,260
这个婴儿的脑部是否接收到了
if the baby's brain is receiving signals

296
00:15:50,260 --> 00:15:52,260
外部世界的信号并加以处理.
and processing the outside world.

297
00:15:55,760 --> 00:15:58,500
乳汁的气味是Donato熟悉的,
Milk is a scent Donato is used to,

298
00:15:58,500 --> 00:16:02,130
所以表现的是他少量的血液流动.
which is why he shows little blood flow.

299
00:16:02,140 --> 00:16:06,440
另一方面,香草的味道是一个令人愉悦的惊喜,
Vanilla,on the other hand,is a pleasant surprise,

300
00:16:06,440 --> 00:16:09,470
会引起脑部血流的剧增.
causing the blood flow in the brain to spike.

301
00:16:09,480 --> 00:16:11,040
[监视器哔哔声]
[ Monitor beeping ]

302
00:16:11,040 --> 00:16:12,880
Lagercrantz: 通过使用香草,这表明
Lagercrantz: With vanilla,it indicates

303
00:16:12,880 --> 00:16:16,180
婴儿对这种味道的反应传入了皮层.
that the baby reacted in the cortex to this smell.

304
00:16:18,080 --> 00:16:22,490
弗里曼：当Hugo给Donato闻一阵有毒气味,比如丙酮,
Freeman: When Hugo gives Donato a whiff of a toxic odor,like acetone,

305
00:16:22,490 --> 00:16:24,820
他就会产生非常强的负面反应,
he has a very negative reaction.

306
00:16:24,830 --> 00:16:26,990
他脑部的血流开始减缓,
His blood flow goes down,

307
00:16:26,990 --> 00:16:31,560
甚至比他闻乳汁气味之前还要低.
even below where he started before he smelled the milk.

308
00:16:31,570 --> 00:16:35,830
这就告诉我们婴儿对于
It tells us that the baby seems to be conscious

309
00:16:35,840 --> 00:16:37,970
好与坏的味道都有意识,
of the good and bad smell,

310
00:16:37,970 --> 00:16:39,700
而这个我认为
which,I think,is very important

311
00:16:39,710 --> 00:16:42,310
从进化的角度来说非常重要,
from an evolutionary point of view.

312
00:16:42,310 --> 00:16:44,040
我的意思是,为了生存,你就必须能够
For survival,I mean,you must be able

313
00:16:44,040 --> 00:16:46,740
辨别什么对你是好的,
to differentiate between what is good for you

314
00:16:46,750 --> 00:16:49,310
而什么是有毒或者对你不好的.
and what is poisonous or not good for you.

315
00:16:51,480 --> 00:16:53,080
弗里曼：Hugo的研究证明
Freeman: Hugo's study proves

316
00:16:53,090 --> 00:16:56,550
一个4天大的婴儿已经拥有了意识,
that even a 4-day-old baby is already conscious,

317
00:16:56,560 --> 00:16:59,860
那么在足月之前呢？
but what about before we reach full-term？

318
00:16:59,860 --> 00:17:02,090
若要检测胎儿的意识
Testing for consciousness in fetuses

319
00:17:02,100 --> 00:17:05,100
使用现有的医疗技术显得过于唐突,
is too invasive with current medical technology,

320
00:17:05,100 --> 00:17:06,830
所以Hugo试图从通过
so Hugo tries to get clues

321
00:17:06,830 --> 00:17:12,170
对22周大的早产儿的研究获得线索.
by studying premature infants born as young as 22 weeks.

322
00:17:12,170 --> 00:17:16,610
我可以说比25、26周大的婴儿
I would say that after 25,26 weeks

323
00:17:16,610 --> 00:17:19,480
似乎都具有了某种程度的意识,
that they seem to have some degree of consciousness,

324
00:17:19,480 --> 00:17:22,580
但在这之前,几乎没有什么迹象
but before that,there are very few signs

325
00:17:22,580 --> 00:17:24,780
表明他们拥有意识.
that they are conscious.

326
00:17:25,950 --> 00:17:29,650
弗里曼：Hugo的研究让他认为
Freeman: Hugo's research has led him to believe

327
00:17:29,660 --> 00:17:34,060
一个婴儿直到25周大的时候才会具有意识.
that a baby cannot be conscious until it is about 25 weeks old.

328
00:17:34,060 --> 00:17:37,230
那么这就是生命开始的时刻吗？
So,is this when life begins？

329
00:17:37,230 --> 00:17:40,730
我认为在意识产生之前,
I think before consciousness has developed,

330
00:17:40,730 --> 00:17:43,300
其实你不能算作一个人类.
you are not a person,actually.

331
00:17:43,300 --> 00:17:46,470
我认为这就是生命诞生的时刻了.
I think this is the time when life begins.

332
00:17:49,480 --> 00:17:51,580
弗里曼：但是一位儿童心理学家认为生命的开始
Freeman: But one child psychologist thinks the beginning of life

333
00:17:51,580 --> 00:17:53,040
来的更晚一些...
comes much later...

334
00:17:54,510 --> 00:17:56,650
...晚到你不可想象.
...later than you could ever imagine.

335
00:18:01,930 --> 00:18:03,530
马克吐温曾经写道,
Mark Twain once wrote,

336
00:18:03,530 --> 00:18:07,330
“人是唯一会脸红的动物,
"Man is the only animal that blushes...

337
00:18:07,330 --> 00:18:09,230
也是唯一需要脸红的动物.”
or needs to."

338
00:18:09,230 --> 00:18:12,670
脸红是人类独特的反应,
Blushing is a uniquely human reaction,

339
00:18:12,670 --> 00:18:14,070
这源自于
one that stems

340
00:18:14,070 --> 00:18:16,510
我们高层次的自我意识,
from our high level of self-awareness,

341
00:18:16,510 --> 00:18:18,840
但是婴儿不会脸红,
but babies don't blush.

342
00:18:18,840 --> 00:18:22,210
脸红是他们需要学习的东西.
It's something they have to learn.

343
00:18:22,210 --> 00:18:28,320
那么人类生命真的仅开始于 我们拥有自我意识的时候吗？
Does human life only truly begin when we become self-conscious？

344
00:18:28,320 --> 00:18:32,190
Philippe Rochat是一位儿童心理学家,
Philippe Rochat is a child psychologist

345
00:18:32,190 --> 00:18:33,760
就职于埃默里大学.
at Emory University.

346
00:18:33,760 --> 00:18:36,360
他的职业生涯就是
He has spent his career

347
00:18:36,360 --> 00:18:40,130
以科学的名义来使自己难堪.
embarrassing himself in the name of science.

348
00:18:40,900 --> 00:18:44,400
如果我把一张标签纸贴在头上,
So,if I have my sticker on the forehead

349
00:18:44,400 --> 00:18:47,740
我看到别人看我,
and I see people giving me looks,okay,

350
00:18:47,740 --> 00:18:50,670
我就会坐立不安开始担忧,
I'm gonna start to be unsettled and concerned.

351
00:18:50,680 --> 00:18:52,880
我的意思是,这就说明我出了什么问题.
I mean,something is wrong about myself.

352
00:18:52,880 --> 00:18:58,080
我认为成为一个人类,就是要关心自己的名誉.
I think that to be human is to be concerned about reputation.

353
00:18:58,080 --> 00:19:00,420
在心理和文化层面上,
At the psychological and cultural level,

354
00:19:00,420 --> 00:19:03,690
人类的生命的确起始于
life begins,indeed,in humans

355
00:19:03,690 --> 00:19:06,620
自我意识产生时--
with the emergence of self-consciousness --

356
00:19:06,620 --> 00:19:08,290
脸红,尴尬和羞愧.
blushing,embarrassment,and shame.

357
00:19:12,530 --> 00:19:15,030
弗里曼：人类拥有发达的社会脑,
Freeman: Humans have developed a social brain.

358
00:19:15,030 --> 00:19:17,200
我们是唯一的
We are the only species

359
00:19:17,200 --> 00:19:20,440
会穿衣、戴珠宝首饰的物种,
that do things like wear clothes and jewelry.

360
00:19:20,440 --> 00:19:22,970
猴子是不化妆的.
Monkeys don't wear makeup.

361
00:19:22,970 --> 00:19:24,840
我们也是唯一的
We are the only species

362
00:19:24,840 --> 00:19:29,980
深刻关注这个世界如何看自己的物种.
that is profoundly concerned with how the world sees us.

363
00:19:29,980 --> 00:19:31,950
根据Philippe的说法,
According to Philippe,

364
00:19:31,950 --> 00:19:35,080
只有当儿童发育出了这种关注,
only when a child develops this concern

365
00:19:35,090 --> 00:19:39,090
才算是一个完全的人,在心理层面上活着.
is he fully human and psychologically alive.

366
00:19:41,860 --> 00:19:42,860
Rochat: OK.
Rochat: Okay.

367
00:19:42,860 --> 00:19:44,430
进来吧.
Come in.

368
00:19:44,430 --> 00:19:48,360
Philippe是埃默里婴幼儿实验室的领导,
Philippe is the head of the Emory Infant and Child Lab,

369
00:19:48,370 --> 00:19:51,770
在这里他尝试检测儿童何时
where he is trying to detect when children become aware

370
00:19:51,770 --> 00:19:54,700
会意识到世界是如何看他们自己的.
of how they are perceived by the world.

371
00:19:54,710 --> 00:19:56,310
你可以坐在这儿. 好的.
You can sit here. Okay.

372
00:19:56,310 --> 00:19:59,980
他说第一个标志,就是当孩子
He says a first sign is when a child feels

373
00:19:59,980 --> 00:20:02,850
在一大群人中会感到压力.
the pressure to go along with a crowd.

374
00:20:02,850 --> 00:20:04,710
来看看一岁大的Booker.
Meet 1-year-old Booker.

375
00:20:04,720 --> 00:20:07,480
嘿,Booker,我们来玩吧.
Hey,Booker,we're gonna play.

376
00:20:07,490 --> 00:20:08,750
Philippe和Booker的母亲
Philippe and Booker's mother

377
00:20:08,750 --> 00:20:10,820
都在额头上贴着粉色标签纸.
have pink stickers on their foreheads.

378
00:20:10,820 --> 00:20:12,860
看--这是goo-goo.
Look -- this is goo-goo.

379
00:20:12,860 --> 00:20:14,320
Rochat: 我们创造了这样社会准则.
Rochat: So,we create this social norm.

380
00:20:14,330 --> 00:20:18,360
然后我们也偷偷的在孩子的头上
Then we place the mark on the kid's head

381
00:20:18,360 --> 00:20:21,130
贴了一个标签纸,
unbeknownst to him,surreptitiously,

382
00:20:21,130 --> 00:20:24,630
然后我们观察当孩子看到
and we look at the child's reaction

383
00:20:24,640 --> 00:20:29,410
自己的头上也有标签纸的反应.
when he sees that he,too,has a mark on the forehead.

384
00:20:31,510 --> 00:20:34,380
弗里曼：如果Booker的脑已经发育出了
Freeman: If Booker's brain has developed an awareness

385
00:20:34,380 --> 00:20:36,150
其他人如何看自己的意识,
of what other people think of him,

386
00:20:36,150 --> 00:20:38,150
他会把标签纸继续贴在头上
he will leave the sticker on his head

387
00:20:38,150 --> 00:20:40,020
来融入其他人...
to fit in with the others...

388
00:20:41,720 --> 00:20:43,420
...但是Booker更在意的是
...but Booker is more concerned

389
00:20:43,420 --> 00:20:45,250
把这个烦人的标签纸从头上拿下来,
with getting the pesky sticker off his head

390
00:20:45,260 --> 00:20:48,890
而不是贴着它融入其他人.
than he is with fitting in.

391
00:20:48,890 --> 00:20:50,430
好的,Kayden,
Okay,Kayden,

392
00:20:50,430 --> 00:20:52,660
在这儿还舒服吗？
are you comfortable there,huh？

393
00:20:52,660 --> 00:20:54,660
但当Philippe在4岁大的Kayden身上
But when Philippe tries the same test

394
00:20:54,670 --> 00:20:56,300
重复这个实验时,
on 4-year-old Kayden,

395
00:20:56,300 --> 00:20:59,500
他看到的是完全不同的反应.
he gets a very different reaction.

396
00:21:03,640 --> 00:21:08,880
Kayden基本上在他自己的图像前不动了,
Kayden basically freezes in front of his own image.

397
00:21:08,880 --> 00:21:13,520
他意识到了我们所有人的头上都有个标签纸,
He noticed that we all have a sticker on the forehead,

398
00:21:13,520 --> 00:21:17,020
于是他把标签纸留在头上.
and he leaves the sticker on.

399
00:21:17,020 --> 00:21:19,390
这就是一致性的想法.
So,there's this idea of conformity.

400
00:21:21,020 --> 00:21:24,090
他们关注自身形象和自我展示,
They care about their own image and self-presentation.

401
00:21:24,100 --> 00:21:28,560
这些现象也会出现在两到三岁大的孩子身上.
This is something that emerges by two to three years of age.

402
00:21:29,330 --> 00:21:31,130
弗里曼：这是个意义重大的里程碑,
Freeman: It's a big milestone,

403
00:21:31,140 --> 00:21:33,140
Philippe的研究表明
but Philippe's research has shown

404
00:21:33,140 --> 00:21:37,640
甚至3岁大的孩子也没有完全的自我意识.
that even a 3-year-old is not yet fully self-aware.

405
00:21:37,640 --> 00:21:38,810
什么？！
What？!

406
00:21:38,810 --> 00:21:41,110
头上有张纸？
Paper on my head？

407
00:21:41,110 --> 00:21:43,080
还有另一个
There is still another level

408
00:21:43,080 --> 00:21:45,880
心理发展的层面需要试验.
of psychological development to reach.

409
00:21:45,880 --> 00:21:48,550
好的,看,我有一些杯子,
Okay. Look. I have some cups here.

410
00:21:48,550 --> 00:21:50,250
我觉得它们真好看.
I think they are beautiful.

411
00:21:50,250 --> 00:21:52,990
这是4岁大的Sidney.
This is 4-year-old Sidney.

412
00:21:52,990 --> 00:21:54,720
我要用它们建造一个东西.
I'm gonna build something with them.

413
00:21:54,730 --> 00:21:57,890
Sidney看着Philippe建造他所谓的
Sidney watches as Philippe builds what he is calling

414
00:21:57,900 --> 00:22:00,630
世界上最美的雕塑.
the most beautiful sculpture in the world.

415
00:22:00,630 --> 00:22:02,060
哇！
Whoa!

416
00:22:02,070 --> 00:22:04,500
但是它很脆弱,对吧？
But it's very fragile,huh？

417
00:22:04,500 --> 00:22:06,800
我不确定这能不能撑得住,
I'm not sure it's gonna hold,

418
00:22:06,800 --> 00:22:11,010
我要去拿些胶水把被子黏在一起.
but I'm gonna get some glue to put the cups together.

419
00:22:11,010 --> 00:22:13,480
所以不要碰它,好么？
So,don't touch it,okay？

420
00:22:13,480 --> 00:22:15,080
我马上就回来.
I'll be right back.

421
00:22:17,810 --> 00:22:20,780
当Sidney耐心等待Philippe拿着胶水回来
As Sidney patiently waits for Philippe to return with glue

422
00:22:20,790 --> 00:22:23,490
加固这个珍贵的艺术作品时,
to secure the precious work of art,

423
00:22:23,490 --> 00:22:27,090
Philippe悄悄地拉动与杯子底部
Philippe secretly pulls a transparent fish wire

424
00:22:27,090 --> 00:22:29,260
相连接的透明钓鱼线.
that is connected to a bottom cup.

425
00:22:35,100 --> 00:22:37,170
问题是,到什么程度
And the question is,is to what extent

426
00:22:37,170 --> 00:22:39,940
孩子会表现出对
the child will show concern

427
00:22:39,940 --> 00:22:42,810
他即将被发现要为
that he's gonna be seen as responsible

428
00:22:42,810 --> 00:22:44,910
倒塌的雕塑负责的担忧.
for the collapsing of the sculpture.

429
00:22:46,080 --> 00:22:48,640
弗里曼：Sidney并不会对杯子神奇的倒塌感到担忧,
Freeman: Sidney is not fazed by the tragic collapse

430
00:22:48,650 --> 00:22:52,180
因为他并不关注自己是否看起来像“罪犯”.
because he does not care if he looks like the culprit.

431
00:22:52,180 --> 00:22:54,050
一切都还好么？
Everything okay？

432
00:22:54,050 --> 00:22:55,720
哦！怎么啦？
Oh! What happened？

433
00:22:55,720 --> 00:22:57,550
你碰它了么？
Did you touch it？

434
00:22:57,560 --> 00:22:58,950
没有.
No.

435
00:22:58,960 --> 00:23:01,490
他不关心Philippe如何看他自己.
He's not concerned with how Philippe will perceive him.

436
00:23:02,790 --> 00:23:04,960
当Philippe在一个5岁大的Milo身上
When Philippe plays the same trick

437
00:23:04,960 --> 00:23:07,060
重复这个实验...
on 5-year-old Milo...

438
00:23:10,700 --> 00:23:12,970
吓死我了.
That was scary.

439
00:23:12,970 --> 00:23:15,840
...他马上开始趁Philippe回来之前
...he takes action and tries to rebuild the pyramid

440
00:23:15,840 --> 00:23:17,070
复原这个杯子建成的金字塔.
before Philippe returns.

441
00:23:21,340 --> 00:23:25,810
Rochat: 我们看到的Milo的行为在其他5岁大的孩子身上
Rochat: And what we've seen with Milo and other 5-year-olds

442
00:23:25,820 --> 00:23:27,220
同样也有体现...
is the great concern...

443
00:23:28,820 --> 00:23:31,790
...他们知道别人对自己的看法和评价,
...about how are they gonna be perceived and judged,

444
00:23:31,790 --> 00:23:33,090
这是
which is a huge step

445
00:23:33,090 --> 00:23:35,460
意识发展的一大步.
in the development of consciousness.

446
00:23:43,200 --> 00:23:45,600
弗里曼：Philippe的试验绘制出了
Freeman: Philippe's tests chart the development

447
00:23:45,600 --> 00:23:48,000
一个具有完全意识的脑的发育图景.
of a fully conscious brain.

448
00:23:48,010 --> 00:23:50,810
就像胎儿分阶段发育一样,
Just as a fetus develops in stages

449
00:23:50,810 --> 00:23:54,780
从一个单细胞,到九个月后的婴儿,
from a single cell to a baby over the course of nine months,

450
00:23:54,780 --> 00:23:57,750
脑中意识的发育同样也是分阶段的.
consciousness in the brain grows in stages,too.

451
00:23:59,880 --> 00:24:03,390
Philippe认为意识的来源需要
Philippe believes consciousness comes to full-term

452
00:24:03,390 --> 00:24:05,150
足月婴儿经历五年的发育...
after about five years...

453
00:24:05,160 --> 00:24:06,490
发生了什么？
What happened？

454
00:24:06,490 --> 00:24:08,420
它自己就倒了.
It just fell by itself.

455
00:24:08,430 --> 00:24:09,890
这怎么可能呢？
How is that possible？

456
00:24:09,890 --> 00:24:14,000
...对于他来说,这正是我们开始真正活着的标志.
...and to him,it's the mark of when we are truly alive.

457
00:24:14,000 --> 00:24:16,470
为什么我们不重新盖起来呢？
Why don't we put it back together？

458
00:24:18,170 --> 00:24:19,740
Rochat: 什么才意味着活着？
Rochat: What it means to be alive？

459
00:24:19,740 --> 00:24:23,870
我觉得活着不是像机器人或机器那样能够运作.
I think it's not to be a robot and not to be a machine.

460
00:24:25,340 --> 00:24:29,240
活着不仅仅是能够感知世界,
To be alive is more than sensing the world,

461
00:24:29,250 --> 00:24:32,410
而是要体会世界.
but to be alive is to feel the world.

462
00:24:35,220 --> 00:24:38,090
说孩子的生命要直到具有自我意识
To say that a child's life does not begin

463
00:24:38,090 --> 00:24:42,760
才算活着有着一个根本上的暗示,
until he becomes self-conscious has radical implications.

464
00:24:42,760 --> 00:24:44,960
一个4岁大的孩子并不算活着,
A 4-year-old is not alive,

465
00:24:44,960 --> 00:24:48,730
但是一个新生的机器也许就是活的...
but a newborn piece of machinery might be...

466
00:24:48,730 --> 00:24:51,870
如果它能够自我思考.
If it can think on its own.

467
00:25:04,410 --> 00:25:07,880
甚至是孩子都能判断这个蜘蛛是活的...
Even a child can tell that a spider is alive...

468
00:25:09,220 --> 00:25:10,990
...而石块不是...
...and a rock is not...

469
00:25:12,120 --> 00:25:16,130
...但是如果让一个无生命的物体变成生物呢？
...but what if a lifeless object became a life-form？

470
00:25:19,560 --> 00:25:23,770
生命会产生于无生命的东西中吗？
Can life begin inside something that's dead？

471
00:25:23,770 --> 00:25:25,770
下午好. 感谢你们的到来.
Good afternoon. Thank you for coming.

472
00:25:25,770 --> 00:25:29,810
我在这里将要对安娜贝拉项目做一个报告.
I'm here to do a presentation on Project Annabelle.

473
00:25:29,810 --> 00:25:33,310
弗里曼：Kate Izhikevich正在解决一系列的任务,
Freeman: Kate Izhikevich is on a serious mission.

474
00:25:33,310 --> 00:25:38,510
她计划制作第一个活着的机器.
She has a plan to build the first ever living machine.

475
00:25:38,520 --> 00:25:41,050
我一直想要一只吉娃娃,而且一直想做的是
I've always wanted a chihuahua,and I've always had a thing

476
00:25:41,050 --> 00:25:42,680
把它命名为--安娜贝拉.
that I wanted to name it -- Annabelle.

477
00:25:42,690 --> 00:25:45,490
弗里曼：她面对的是最严厉的批评家--
Freeman: She is facing her toughest critic yet --

478
00:25:45,490 --> 00:25:48,820
著名的计算机神经学家
renowned computational neuroscientist

479
00:25:48,830 --> 00:25:53,700
Eugene Izhikevich,也是她的爸爸.
Eugene Izhikevich,also known as dad.

480
00:25:53,700 --> 00:25:56,830
我决定如果制作一只机器狗会怎么样？
I decided that,what if I could make a dog that was robotic？

481
00:25:56,830 --> 00:25:59,330
Eugene: Kate从三岁的时候就开始
Eugene: Kate has been asking me for a real dog

482
00:25:59,340 --> 00:26:01,770
向我要一只真正的狗,
since she was three years old,

483
00:26:01,770 --> 00:26:03,270
过去两年中,
and for the last two years,

484
00:26:03,270 --> 00:26:05,540
她则向我要一只机器狗.
she has been asking me for a robotic dog.

485
00:26:05,540 --> 00:26:07,380
所以,不易弄脏,它吃的和排泄出的都是电池.
So,no mess. It eats and poops out batteries.

486
00:26:07,380 --> 00:26:10,210
Kate认为安娜贝拉项目是可能实现的
Kate thinks that Project Annabelle is possible

487
00:26:10,210 --> 00:26:12,350
因为我是做计算机神经科学的,
because I do computational neuroscience

488
00:26:12,350 --> 00:26:15,380
我已经给机器人制作了人工神经系统.
and I build artificial nervous systems for robots.

489
00:26:15,390 --> 00:26:16,720
Kate: 我希望安娜贝拉项目
Kate: I hope Project Annabelle

490
00:26:16,720 --> 00:26:18,990
会是我爸爸的首要任务.
will become my dad's first priority.

491
00:26:18,990 --> 00:26:19,960
谢谢.
Thank you.

492
00:26:22,890 --> 00:26:27,000
弗里曼：Kate认为她爸爸是这项工作的最好人选.
Freeman: Kate thinks her dad is the best man for the job.

493
00:26:27,000 --> 00:26:29,970
Eugene制造了人脑的最详尽的
Eugene has built the most detailed computer model

494
00:26:29,970 --> 00:26:31,530
计算机模型--
of the human brain --

495
00:26:31,540 --> 00:26:33,270
一千亿个神经元
100 billion neurons

496
00:26:33,270 --> 00:26:36,770
和几乎10的15次方个突触.
and almost 1 quadrillion synapses.

497
00:26:36,770 --> 00:26:41,280
他的终极目标是在
His ultimate goal is to create consciousness

498
00:26:41,280 --> 00:26:43,780
人工神经系统中创造意识.
in an artificial nervous system.

499
00:26:43,780 --> 00:26:45,910
他认为这会是一种新型生物的开端,
He thinks it could be the beginning

500
00:26:45,920 --> 00:26:50,120
而这种生物在地球上从未出现过.
of a new life-form,one never before seen on earth.

501
00:26:50,120 --> 00:26:53,260
我不认为意识只是
I don't believe that consciousness is something

502
